+# GOAL — block 27: the unsoldered formation law at weak coupling — the causal coupling contracts, one invariant law, exponential loss of memory for `β < 1/√3` (2026-09-16)
+
+**Directive.** Owner 2026-09-16: "keep running the campaign for 12 more hours." Block 26 executed the strong-coupling side of the sphere formation law (memory lost algebraically, simulated) and proved its linear theory. The weak-coupling side is proof-shaped and completes the campaign's four-way table (static/formation × six-axis/sphere) of explicit uniqueness regions: six-axis static (block 03), six-axis formation (block 08, `3c < 1`), sphere static (block 21, `β < √3/6`), sphere formation — this block.
+
+**Exact target (claim type `bounded_theorem`).** For the sphere formation law in level order (block 26's declared objects):
+- T1 (the sensitivity of the kernel): for `V, V' ∈ R³`, `TV(K_V, K_{V'}) ≤ |V − V'|/(2√3)`, where `K_V(ds) = (|V|/(4π sinh|V|)) e^{V·s} dσ`; hence `W_1(K_V, K_{V'}) ≤ |V − V'|/√3` for the chordal distance. Ingredients: the exponential-family identity `Var(w) = A'(κ)`; the directional second moment `E[((s − A(κ)û)·d)²] = A/κ + (1 − 3A/κ − A²)(û·d)²`; the sign lemma `1 − 3A/κ − A² ≤ 0` (equivalently `A(κ)/κ` is decreasing), proved by comparing the series of `2 sinh² κ` and `(κ/2) sinh 2κ + κ²` term by term; `A/κ ≤ 1/3`.
+- T2 (the causal coupling contracts): from two initial planes, coupling each site's record optimally given the coupled predecessors, the per-site expected chordal distance obeys `D_{t+1} ≤ √3 β D_t`; for `β < 1/√3` the level automaton has a unique invariant law, invariant under rotations, and the law of any level from any initial plane converges to it exponentially in the per-site Wasserstein distance.
+- T3 (exponential loss of memory): from the aligned plane, `m_t ≤ (√3β)^t` for `β < 1/√3` (the antipodal comparison); on one site the exact rate is `A(3β)^t`, and `A(3β) ≤ β`.
+- T4 (the reach of the route): the mean map `F(V) = A(|V|)V̂` has `|F(V) − F(V')| ≥ (|V − V'|/3)(1 − O(|V|²))`, so no coupling argument of this kind proves contraction beyond `β = 1`; numerically the total-variation sensitivity is close to `|V − V'|/4` (route reach `β < 2/3`), the proved constant `1/(2√3)` sits between.
+- Executed: the series coefficients exactly; the identities symbolically; the total-variation bound against quadrature at random configurations; the magnetization simulated at `β = 0.3, 0.5` against `(√3β)^t` and `β^t`.
+
+**Lens pass.** *"Another uniqueness region by the same coupling as block 08."* Yes, and that is the point: the fourth cell of the table with an explicit constant. *"Is `1/√3` sharp?"* No: T4 says the route can reach at most `β = 1`, quadrature suggests `2/3` for the total-variation version; the true loss of memory is at every `β` (block 26, executed). *"Names."* The exponential family and total variation are standard; author names only in the allowed sections.
+
+**Forbidden phrases:** the standing list plus "has no ordered phase", "does not order", "converge", "critical", "certified".
